A||||3||Gold-86SE||Top headline: Fieros help make new friends, film at 11.||||6||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||06-08-2000||11:05 AM||||<BR>I have to relay a really cool Fiero story to you guys regarding what happened to my family and me after church Wednesday night.  It is one of those stories where everything is perfect and hitting on all cylinders.<P>We left the building and we were driving home in the family vehicle, a humdrum Dodge Caravan.  It was twilight, so it was somewhat difficult to be certain on the make/model of the car that was driving toward us.<P>I thought to myself "Hey, that looks like a Fiero, naa, yeah it is!"  And then said to my wife,  "Man, I'm tempted to follow him and find out who he is."   If he owns a Fiero, he has to be smart, right?<P>With some minor persuasion from my wife I began a slow speed persuit of this Fiero, which turned out to be a Red '88 automatic 4-cyl Coupe.  I must have followed him for about 3 or 4 miles, before I flagged him down and spoke with him, and during the persuit my son even got in on the action by saying with his 4-year old voice "Come on Fiero, pull over."<P>I was able to flag him down at a stop sign, asked his female passenger if we could chat a few minutes, and she said "sure."<P>Well, to make a somewhat long story short, we ended up talking about 15-20 minutes about... can you guess? Yep, Fieros.  Turns out this gentleman, fell in love with them around 1990 and has owned, I think he said, somewhere around 40 Fieros, and I thought my 4 or 5 was a lot   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Momentary lapse there, I forgot a Fiero owner can never have enough Fieros.<P>Found out that he even manages the Austin Fiero Club website: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Track/1994/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Track/1994/index.html</A>  <P>Conclusion:<BR>I have discovered a beautiful thing, every Fiero owner I have met has been nice and very enthusiastic regarding the Fiero.  The only real buggaboo about Fiero ownership is that it can become very intoxicating, were you think, eat, sleep, and breathe FIERO.  Man, what a car.<P>Just as a side note, I sure am glad he stopped because I could have very easily been some quack with malivent intent, which thankfully I am not.  I am just your average ordinary Fiero Fanatic.<P>Oh, yeah, told him about fiero.nl, can't pass up any opportunity to hand out info about PFF.<P>BTW, this little red '88 is for sale, and if anyone is interested, I have his email address.<P>Sorry, had some grammer problems.<p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 06-08-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||ChadMan||06-08-2000||02:06 PM||||Great story!  Almost every Fiero driver you meet is cool.  <P>I was out riding around on my little riceburner motorcycle the other night and stopped for gas.  The guy at the next pump had a beautiful 85 SE.  I saw him looking at my bike, and he saw me looking at his car, so we started shooting the bull.  Turns out he was the original owner - the SE was a 2M6 4-speed with original beautiful red paint and gray interior.  He said his wife keeps wanting him to sell his Fiero but he never will.  When I told him I have owned three Fieros, he laughed and said he'd owned two CB350 Hondas just like mine!<P>I gave him the URL for the Forum and rode away.  Maybe we'll see him on here sometime.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||06-08-2000||04:23 PM||||Last year I went to the FOCOA nationals in Columbus, OH and had the BEST time.  Unfortunately my Formula couldn't make it due to a few problems, but I went anyway.  I was also buying a set of rims for my other car in town from someone else, but thats another story.  Anyway, everybody there was great!  Fiero owners are some of the best people I have known.  I am hoping I can make it to FOCOA show this year with my Formula.  I am also surprised by the number of people I meet that don't know it is mid engined.  Showing them the engine in the trunk really peaks their interests.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||06-08-2000||04:38 PM||||About 6 weeks ago I was driving downtown, and I saw a Fiero.. I figured it was one of the 84 coupes in my town, but as I passed I saw it was a SE.. so I parked and walked back to look at it..<P>it was 99.999% mint... I saw a small scratch on the rear bumper, but it was amazing... even the wheel wells were clean... so I waited an HOUR in front of the library to talk to the guy. when he finally walked up to his car, I sorta introduced myself, said that hiw car was the nicest fiero I'd ever seen, and that I had been driving by and stopped to take a look. I mentioned the forum, but the guy basically brushed me off.. said "they're probably all american"..<P>right, I forgot that a canadian Fiero is totally different than an american one....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>anyway, either he was in a big hurry and didn't bother to tell me, or he simply didn't want to talk. for whatever reason, he didn't seem too nice. However that's the exception.. all the other Fiero owners I've met have been very cool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
